  "output": "## Performance Analysis — Canon (Next.js 16 / React 19 / Drizzle / Postgres)\n\nScope note up front: this is a small, deliberately-architected app (~100 source files, mostly Server Components). The data layer already avoids the classic N+1 traps on purpose — the code comments literally say so (\"Three round trips, not three hundred\"). So rather than inventing N+1s that aren't there, here's what's actually real, in the order you asked for.\n\n---\n\n### 1. N+1 query patterns — none found (worth confirming, not inventing)\n\n`lib/server/conflicts.ts`, `export.ts`, `entities.ts` all batch correctly:\n- `getQueuePage` runs rows/counts/total in `Promise.all`, then does **one** grouped query for child counts across the whole page (`countChildrenByParent`) instead of a query per row.\n- `getQueueCounts` computes both facet counts (status, severity) in 2 grouped queries, not per-chip queries.\n\nThis part of the codebase is already better than most production apps. No changes needed here.\n\n---\n\n### 2. The real database issue: missing indexes on every hot path\n\n`lib/db/schema.ts` defines **one** index in the entire schema (`entities_run_source_external_idx`). Postgres does **not** auto-index foreign keys, and nearly every query in `lib/server/*.ts` filters or joins on an unindexed FK or sorts on an unindexed timestamp:\n\n| Table.column | Used by | Current index |\n|---|---|---|\n| `conflicts.runId` | every conflict-queue query, export, severity breakdown | none |\n| `resolutions.conflictId` | `LEFT JOIN` in nearly every conflicts query | none |\n| `matches.entityAId` / `entityBId` | `baseQuery()` join | none |\n| `entities.parentExternalId` (+ `runId`) | `countChildrenByParent` | none (existing composite index is on `runId, sourceId, externalId` — doesn't cover this filter) |\n| `runs.startedAt` | `ORDER BY desc(runs.startedAt)` on every runs-list load | none |\n| `audit_log.runId`, `audit_log.createdAt` | `listRunAudit`, `getAuditPage` | none |\n| `resolutions.status` | `inArray` filter + `groupBy` in queue counts | none |\n\nAt current data volumes this won't be visible. It will bite hard the moment a run produces thousands of conflicts — every `conflicts.runId = ?` filter degenerates to a sequential scan, and the `LEFT JOIN resolutions` on an unindexed FK is the single most expensive part of `getQueuePage`.\n\n```ts\n// lib/db/schema.ts — add alongside the existing entities index\nexport const conflicts = pgTable(\"conflicts\", {\n  // ...existing columns\n}, (table) => [\n  index(\"conflicts_run_id_idx\").on(table.runId),\n  index(\"conflicts_match_id_idx\").on(table.matchId),\n]);\n\nexport const resolutions = pgTable(\"resolutions\", {\n  // ...existing columns\n}, (table) => [\n  index(\"resolutions_conflict_id_idx\").on(table.conflictId),\n  index(\"resolutions_status_idx\").on(table.status),\n]);\n\nexport const matches = pgTable(\"matches\", {\n  // ...existing columns\n}, (table) => [\n  index(\"matches_entity_a_id_idx\").on(table.entityAId),\n  index(\"matches_entity_b_id_idx\").on(table.entityBId),\n]);\n\nexport const entities = pgTable(\"entities\", {\n  // ...existing columns\n}, (table) => [\n  index(\"entities_run_source_external_idx\").on(table.runId, table.sourceId, table.externalId),\n  index(\"entities_run_parent_idx\").on(table.runId, table.parentExternalId), // covers countChildrenByParent\n]);\n\nexport const runs = pgTable(\"runs\", {\n  // ...existing columns\n}, (table) => [index(\"runs_started_at_idx\").on(table.startedAt)]);\n\nexport const auditLog = pgTable(\"audit_log\", {\n  // ...existing columns\n}, (table) => [index(\"audit_log_run_created_idx\").on(table.runId, table.createdAt)]);\n```\n\nAlso worth a unique index the code itself flags as missing: `lib/server/conflicts.ts:62-66` explicitly notes `resolutions.conflictId` should be unique but isn't enforced — if the engine ever double-writes, `getQueuePage` will silently double-count rows.\n\nThis is the single highest-leverage change in the codebase — run `db:generate` + `db:migrate` after adding these.\n\n---\n\n### 3. React re-renders\n\n**a) `ConflictDenseTable` re-renders all 100 rows on every selection change.**\n`components/conflicts/conflict-dense-table.tsx:29-164` maps over up to `QUEUE_PAGE_SIZE = 100` rows (`types/queue.ts:134`). Selecting a row only changes `expanded`/`aria-expanded` for (at most) two rows, but since no row is memoized, every keystroke of `j`/`k` re-renders and re-diffs all 100 row subtrees (8 cells each) purely to flip a boolean on two of them.\n\n```tsx\n// conflict-dense-table.tsx\nconst Row = memo(function Row({ row, expanded, status, onSelect, staggerIndex, ... }) {\n  // body of the current inline row renderer\n});\n\n// in ConflictDenseTable, per row:\n<Row\n  key={row.conflictId}\n  row={row}\n  expanded={index === selectedIndex}\n  status={decided[row.conflictId]?.status ?? row.resolution?.status ?? null}\n  onSelect={() => onSelect(index)}   // stays inline unless you also useCallback-per-row\n  staggerIndex={staggerIndex(index)}\n/>\n```\n`row`, `staggerIndex(index)`, and `status` are all stable/primitive per row already, so `React.memo` with default shallow comparison will actually skip re-render for the 98 untouched rows. The `onSelect` closure is the one thing that'll defeat memoization if left inline — either accept that (cheap arrow fn creation is fine) or pass `index` down and call `onSelect(index)` inside the memoized row using a stable `onSelect` reference.\n\n**b) `RulesetEditor` re-sorts + re-`indexOf`s on every keystroke.**\n`components/rules/ruleset-editor.tsx:61-67` and the render body:\n```ts\nconst ordered = orderRulesByEvaluation(rules);   // O(n log n), every render\n...\n{ordered.map((rule) => {\n  const index = rules.indexOf(rule);              // O(n) per row → O(n²) total\n  ...\n  position={ordered.indexOf(rule) + 1}             // another O(n) per row\n```\nTyping a single character into any rule's `field` input re-runs this whole O(n²) pass. Ruleset sizes are small today, so this is invisible in practice, but it's free to fix and will matter if rulesets grow:\n```ts\nconst ordered = useMemo(() => orderRulesByEvaluation(rules), [rules]);\nconst idByRule = useMemo(() => new Map(rules.map((r, i) => [r.id, i])), [rules]);\n// use idByRule.get(rule.id) instead of rules.indexOf(rule)\n// and track position via ordered's own index instead of ordered.indexOf(rule)\n```\n\n**c) Everything else is clean.** `ConflictQueue`'s keyboard handler, `useReview`, `RunLive`'s polling — callbacks are properly memoized with `useCallback`/`useMemo` where it matters, and the `key={queueKey(...)}` remount-on-filter-change pattern (`app/(console)/conflicts/page.tsx:101`) is a genuinely good technique for avoiding stale-selection bugs without extra effects.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Caching opportunities\n\n**a) No HTTP caching on read-only route handlers.** `app/api/conflicts/route.ts`, `app/api/rulesets/route.ts`, `app/api/sources/route.ts` all return `Response.json(...)` with no `Cache-Control` header. Given this is session-protected internal data that changes via review actions, `no-store` is the *correct* default — but for the specific case of `GET /api/conflicts` hit by an \"ops script\" (the route's own doc comment says that's its purpose), a short `private, max-age=2-5` would let repeated polling scripts avoid hammering Postgres without risking staleness a human would notice.\n\n**b) `RunLive` polling interval is reasonable but fixed.** `components/runs/run-live.tsx:17` polls every 2s regardless of run size/duration. Fine as-is (the comment already reasons about the 300-request budget for a 10-minute run), no change needed — flagging only because it's the one place actually worth watching if run durations grow.\n\n**c) Ruleset/source lookups are cheap today but not request-deduped.** `listSources()` / `listRulesets()` (`lib/server/sources.ts`, `rulesets.ts`) are plain async functions, not wrapped in React's `cache()`. Currently each route only calls them once, so there's no live bug — but if a shared layout or nested component ever calls `listSources()` independently of a page that already did, that becomes two DB round trips per request. Since these are read constantly and change rarely, wrapping them is cheap insurance:\n```ts\nimport { cache } from \"react\";\nexport const listSources = cache(async (): Promise<Source[]> => {\n  await connection();\n  const rows = await getDb().select().from(sources).orderBy(sources.createdAt);\n  return rows.map((row) => sourceSchema.parse(row));\n});\n```\n`React.cache` dedupes within a single request/render pass only (not across requests), so this is zero-risk for a \"must always be fresh\" internal tool.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Memory leaks\n\nNone found. This is genuinely clean:\n- `run-live.tsx`'s `setInterval` for the elapsed-time clock has a proper `clearInterval` cleanup (`:106-107`), gated correctly on the `running` flag.\n- `conflict-queue.tsx`'s `window.addEventListener(\"keydown\", ...)` is removed on unmount (`:130-131`).\n- `conflict-focus.tsx`'s `scrollIntoView` effect has no subscriptions to leak.\n- The TanStack `QueryClient` in `query-provider.tsx` is created once via `useState(() => new QueryClient(...))` scoped to the one component that mounts it, and unmounts with it — no root-level provider leaking state across route changes (the file's own comment explains this was a deliberate choice).\n\nNothing to fix here.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Redundant computations\n\nBeyond the `RulesetEditor` case above (§3b), two smaller ones:\n\n**`ExportPanel`'s byte estimate recomputation is fine** (`useMemo` correctly used, `components/runs/export-panel.tsx:66-73`) — flagging only as a positive example, not a problem.\n\n**`getExportSizing` and `buildRunExport` both re-derive counts/rows independently** (`lib/server/export.ts:122-160` and `:311-319`) — if a user opens the export panel (which calls sizing) and then downloads (which calls `buildRunExport`), the decision/orphan rows are queried twice across two requests. This is unavoidable given they're separate HTTP requests (preview vs. download) and the data must be fresh at download time, so it's not really fixable without accepting staleness — not flagging as an actual defect, just noting it's the one place doing real duplicate work by necessity.\n\n---\n\n## Priority order\n\n1. **Add the missing indexes** (§2) — highest impact, zero behavior change, becomes urgent the moment run sizes grow past a few hundred conflicts.\n2. **Memoize `ConflictDenseTable` rows** (§3a) — matters once queues are consistently near the 100-row page cap.\n3. Everything else (§3b, §4c) is cheap-to-fix but low urgency at current scale — reasonable to defer until they show up in a profiler.\n",
